  // ── Content: Patent Trivia ────────────────────────────────────────
  var PATENT_TRIVIA = [
    { q: "A US utility patent's term runs…", a: ["20 years from the earliest filing date", "17 years from grant", "25 years from publication"], c: 0, x: "Since 1995 (TRIPS/URAA): 20 years from filing, not 17 from grant — which killed the 'submarine patent' strategy of keeping applications pending for decades." },
    { q: "The first US patent (1790) was examined and signed by…", a: ["Thomas Jefferson (and signed by George Washington)", "Benjamin Franklin", "Alexander Hamilton"], c: 0, x: "Jefferson personally examined early patents as Secretary of State. Patent No. X1 went to Samuel Hopkins for an improved potash process." },
    { q: "A provisional patent application expires after…", a: ["12 months", "18 months", "5 years"], c: 0, x: "It's a placeholder, never examined. Miss the 12-month conversion window and the priority date evaporates — the classic startup catastrophe." },
    { q: "The America Invents Act (2013) switched the US to…", a: ["First-inventor-to-file", "First-to-invent", "First-to-publish"], c: 0, x: "Ending two centuries of first-to-invent. The race to the Patent Office now matters more than the lab notebook." },
    { q: "Under §101, the four categories of patentable subject matter are process, machine, manufacture, and…", a: ["Composition of matter", "Business method", "Abstract idea"], c: 0, x: "Abstract ideas, laws of nature, and natural phenomena are the judicially-created exceptions — the battleground of Alice and Mayo." },
    { q: "A design patent's term is…", a: ["15 years from grant", "20 years from filing", "10 years from publication"], c: 0, x: "15 years from grant for applications filed after May 2015 (14 before that). No maintenance fees — unlike utility patents." },
    { q: "A 'Markman hearing' determines…", a: ["How the claims are construed — by the judge", "Damages — by the jury", "Inventorship disputes"], c: 0, x: "Markman v. Westview (1996): claim construction is a question of law for the court. Cases are often won or lost at this hearing." },
    { q: "The doctrine of equivalents lets a patentee reach…", a: ["Products outside the literal claim language", "Foreign sales", "Pre-issuance conduct"], c: 0, x: "Insubstantial differences don't avoid infringement — but prosecution history estoppel (what you gave up to get the patent) limits the doctrine's reach." },
    { q: "US applications generally publish…", a: ["18 months after the earliest filing date", "Immediately on filing", "Only when granted"], c: 0, x: "The 18-month publication rule — why patent attorneys joke they can't keep secrets. Non-publication requests exist but waive foreign filing." },
    { q: "Edison's lifetime count of US patents was roughly…", a: ["1,093", "350", "5,000"], c: 0, x: "A record for over a century — since lapped by modern prolific inventors like Shunpei Yamazaki and Lowell Wood, each past 1,300." },
    { q: "The US grace period lets an inventor file within…", a: ["1 year of their own public disclosure", "30 days of any disclosure", "5 years of conception"], c: 0, x: "One year — and it's a US peculiarity. Most of the world has absolute novelty: publish first, lose your foreign rights immediately." },
    { q: "An obviousness-type double patenting rejection is typically cured by…", a: ["A terminal disclaimer", "Paying a surcharge", "Canceling the spec"], c: 0, x: "The disclaimer ties the second patent's term (and ownership) to the first — you can extend your claim coverage but not your monopoly clock." },
    { q: "A PCT application gives you roughly how long to enter national phase?", a: ["30 months from priority", "12 months from filing", "5 years from publication"], c: 0, x: "30 (sometimes 31) months — the PCT is a delay-and-decide machine: one filing keeps ~150 countries open while you find money and markets." },
    { q: "'Means for fastening' in a claim invokes…", a: ["§112(f) — construed as the spec's structure + equivalents", "Automatic indefiniteness", "Broadest possible scope"], c: 0, x: "Means-plus-function claiming sounds broad but is cabined to what the specification actually discloses — a classic drafting trap." },
    { q: "The Wright brothers' patent war is often blamed for…", a: ["Stalling early US aviation until a WWI patent pool", "The invention of the jet engine", "The creation of the USPTO"], c: 0, x: "Litigation so fierce the US government forced a cross-licensing pool in 1917. Lesson: aggressive enforcement can freeze an industry." },
    { q: "Gordon Gould's fight over the laser patent lasted about…", a: ["30 years", "3 years", "6 months"], c: 0, x: "He noted 'LASER' in a 1957 notebook but lost the early filings race; decades of interference battles later, he finally collected royalties in the late 1980s." },
    { q: "35 U.S.C. §102 governs which patentability requirement?", a: ["Novelty", "Non-obviousness", "Written description"], c: 0, x: "§102 defines prior art and novelty. Obviousness lives in §103, and the disclosure requirements in §112." },
    { q: "Non-obviousness is codified in which section of Title 35?", a: ["§103", "§101", "§112"], c: 0, x: "§103 asks whether the claimed invention as a whole would have been obvious to a person of ordinary skill in the art." },
    { q: "Written description, enablement and definiteness all live in…", a: ["§112", "§102", "§103"], c: 0, x: "§112(a) covers written description, enablement and best mode; §112(b) requires claims that particularly point out and distinctly claim the invention." },
    { q: "After the AIA, which §112(a) requirement can no longer invalidate an issued patent?", a: ["Best mode", "Enablement", "Written description"], c: 0, x: "Applicants must still disclose the best mode, but the AIA removed failure to do so as a ground for invalidity or unenforceability (§282)." },
    { q: "Patent infringement is defined in which section?", a: ["§271", "§284", "§102"], c: 0, x: "§271(a) covers making, using, offering to sell, selling or importing; §271(b) and (c) cover induced and contributory infringement." },
    { q: "Patent damages 'adequate to compensate' are governed by…", a: ["§284", "§285", "§271"], c: 0, x: "§284 sets a floor of a reasonable royalty and lets the court increase damages up to three times the amount found." },
    { q: "Halo Electronics v. Pulse (2016) changed the law on…", a: ["Enhanced damages for willful infringement", "Patent venue", "Patent-eligible subject matter"], c: 0, x: "The Court scrapped the Federal Circuit's rigid two-part Seagate test and restored district court discretion to punish egregious misconduct." },
    { q: "Attorney fees in 'exceptional cases' come from…", a: ["§285", "§284", "§271(e)"], c: 0, x: "§285 lets courts award reasonable attorney fees to the prevailing party in exceptional cases — patentee or accused infringer." },
    { q: "Octane Fitness v. ICON (2014) concerned…", a: ["When a case is 'exceptional' for fee awards", "Patent-eligible subject matter", "Venue for patent suits"], c: 0, x: "An exceptional case is simply one that stands out from others; the Court rejected the Federal Circuit's stricter Brooks Furniture test." },
    { q: "Under §286, damages generally cannot reach back more than…", a: ["Six years before the complaint", "Two years before the complaint", "Ten years before the complaint"], c: 0, x: "§286 is a damages lookback, not a statute of limitations — the suit itself can be filed any time, but only the last six years count." },
    { q: "SCA Hygiene v. First Quality (2017) held that laches…", a: ["Cannot bar damages within §286's six-year window", "Bars any suit filed after two years", "Applies only to design patents"], c: 0, x: "Following Petrella (copyright), the Court held Congress already set the timeliness rule, leaving no room for laches as a damages bar." },
    { q: "Patent marking and its effect on damages is addressed by…", a: ["§287", "§292", "§101"], c: 0, x: "Failure to mark a patented article limits damages to the period after actual notice. Since the AIA, 'virtual marking' via a website is allowed." },
    { q: "After the AIA, who can sue for false patent marking under §292?", a: ["The US or a party with competitive injury", "Anyone, as a qui tam relator", "Only the USPTO"], c: 0, x: "Pre-AIA qui tam suits over expired patent numbers flooded the courts; the AIA cut standing back to those actually injured." },
    { q: "An issued US patent is presumed valid under…", a: ["§282", "§101", "§131"], c: 0, x: "Each claim is presumed valid independently of the others, and the party asserting invalidity bears the burden of proof." },
    { q: "Microsoft v. i4i (2011) held invalidity in court must be proven by…", a: ["Clear and convincing evidence", "A preponderance of the evidence", "Proof beyond a reasonable doubt"], c: 0, x: "Unanimous — even when the prior art was never considered by the examiner, though that fact may carry weight with the jury." },
    { q: "In an IPR, the petitioner must prove unpatentability by…", a: ["A preponderance of the evidence", "Clear and convincing evidence", "Proof beyond a reasonable doubt"], c: 0, x: "§316(e) sets a lower burden than district court and there is no presumption of validity — a big reason IPR became so popular." },
    { q: "IPR petitions may challenge claims only on grounds of…", a: ["§102/§103 based on patents and printed publications", "Any ground, including §101", "§112 indefiniteness only"], c: 0, x: "§311(b) limits IPR's scope. Eligibility, §112 and public-use challenges must go to post-grant review or court." },
    { q: "Post-grant review (PGR) must be requested within…", a: ["9 months of grant", "1 year of grant", "30 days of grant"], c: 0, x: "PGR allows any invalidity ground — including §101 and §112 — but only for first-inventor-to-file patents and only in that nine-month window." },
    { q: "A party sued for infringement must file any IPR petition within…", a: ["1 year of being served with the complaint", "6 months of the complaint", "3 years of the complaint"], c: 0, x: "The §315(b) time bar. Miss it and IPR is off the table for that petitioner, its real parties in interest and privies." },
    { q: "By statute, the PTAB's IPR final written decision is due…", a: ["Within 1 year of institution", "Within 6 months of filing", "Within 3 years of filing"], c: 0, x: "§316(a)(11) sets the one-year deadline, extendable by up to six months for good cause." },
    { q: "IPR estoppel under §315(e) covers grounds the petitioner…", a: ["Raised or reasonably could have raised", "Actually won on", "Mentioned in a footnote"], c: 0, x: "After a final written decision, the petitioner can't reassert those grounds at the PTO, in district court or at the ITC." },
    { q: "Oil States v. Greene's Energy (2018) held that IPR…", a: ["Does not violate Article III or the Seventh Amendment", "Is unconstitutional", "Requires a jury trial"], c: 0, x: "Granting patents is a matter of public rights, so Congress may let the PTO reconsider its own grants without an Article III court." },
    { q: "SAS Institute v. Iancu (2018) held that once the PTAB institutes IPR, it must…", a: ["Decide every challenged claim", "Pick only the strongest claims", "Stay for district court"], c: 0, x: "No more partial institution: the final written decision must address all claims the petitioner challenged." },
    { q: "United States v. Arthrex (2021) concerned…", a: ["The Appointments Clause and PTAB judges", "Patent-eligible subject matter", "Patent venue"], c: 0, x: "Unreviewable APJ decisions were incompatible with their status as inferior officers; the fix was to let the USPTO Director review final decisions." },
    { q: "Since November 2018, the PTAB construes claims in IPR using…", a: ["The Phillips standard used in court", "Broadest reasonable interpretation", "Dictionary meaning only"], c: 0, x: "Cuozzo (2016) had upheld broadest reasonable interpretation, but the PTO switched to Phillips by rule to align with district courts." },
    { q: "Phillips v. AWH (Fed. Cir. 2005, en banc) called which source the best guide to claim meaning?", a: ["The specification", "General dictionaries", "Expert testimony"], c: 0, x: "Intrinsic evidence — claims, specification, prosecution history — comes first; dictionaries and experts are less significant extrinsic evidence." },
    { q: "Teva v. Sandoz (2015) held subsidiary fact-findings in claim construction are reviewed for…", a: ["Clear error", "De novo", "Abuse of discretion"], c: 0, x: "Ultimate construction stays a question of law, but the Federal Circuit must defer to the trial judge's findings on extrinsic evidence." },
    { q: "Nautilus v. Biosig (2014) set the definiteness test as…", a: ["Reasonable certainty to skilled artisans", "'Not insolubly ambiguous'", "Any plausible construction"], c: 0, x: "The Court rejected the Federal Circuit's lenient 'insolubly ambiguous' test. The patent involved heart-rate monitors on exercise equipment." },
    { q: "Amgen v. Sanofi (2023) held that a genus claim must enable…", a: ["Its full scope", "Just one working example", "Only the preferred embodiment"], c: 0, x: "A unanimous Court invalidated functional antibody claims covering potentially millions of antibodies that the spec did not teach how to make." },
    { q: "Ariad v. Eli Lilly (Fed. Cir. 2010, en banc) confirmed that §112(a) contains…", a: ["A written description requirement separate from enablement", "Only an enablement requirement", "No disclosure requirement"], c: 0, x: "The spec must show the inventor actually possessed the claimed invention — teaching how to make it is not enough." },
