    { q: "Mayo v. Prometheus (2012) invalidated claims about…", a: ["Adjusting thiopurine doses from metabolite levels", "Isolated DNA sequences", "Hedging energy prices"], c: 0, x: "The correlation between metabolite levels and efficacy was a law of nature, and the remaining steps were well-understood, routine and conventional." },
    { q: "AMP v. Myriad Genetics (2013) held that isolated naturally occurring DNA is…", a: ["Not patent-eligible", "Patent-eligible", "Eligible only once sequenced"], c: 0, x: "Isolating a gene doesn't create anything new. Myriad's patents covered the BRCA1 and BRCA2 breast-cancer genes." },
    { q: "In Myriad, which form of DNA remained patent-eligible?", a: ["cDNA", "Genomic DNA", "Mitochondrial DNA"], c: 0, x: "Synthetic complementary DNA omits the non-coding introns, so it is not a product of nature." },
    { q: "Bilski v. Kappos (2010) involved a claimed method of…", a: ["Hedging risk in commodities trading", "Curing synthetic rubber", "Detecting fetal DNA"], c: 0, x: "Abstract idea, held unpatentable — but the Court refused to bar business methods categorically." },
    { q: "Diamond v. Chakrabarty (1980) concerned…", a: ["A genetically engineered oil-eating bacterium", "A rubber-curing process", "A hybrid corn seed"], c: 0, x: "5-4: a live, human-made microorganism is patentable subject matter. The case opened the door to the biotech industry." },
    { q: "Chakrabarty's famous line 'anything under the sun that is made by man' came from…", a: ["The 1952 Act's legislative history", "Thomas Jefferson's letters", "The Constitution"], c: 0, x: "The Court quoted the committee reports accompanying the 1952 Patent Act to show Congress intended §101 to be read broadly." },
    { q: "Diamond v. Diehr (1981) upheld a claim to…", a: ["A rubber-curing process using the Arrhenius equation", "A pure mathematical algorithm", "A method of hedging risk"], c: 0, x: "A process applying a formula to transform material is eligible even though the formula itself is not." },
    { q: "Gottschalk v. Benson (1972) rejected a claim to…", a: ["Converting binary-coded decimals to pure binary", "Curing rubber in a mold", "Updating alarm limits"], c: 0, x: "Allowing it would wholly preempt a mathematical formula — effectively patenting the algorithm itself." },
    { q: "Parker v. Flook (1978) involved…", a: ["Updating alarm limits in catalytic conversion", "Converting BCD numerals to binary", "An oil-eating bacterium"], c: 0, x: "Insignificant post-solution activity cannot transform an unpatentable formula into a patentable process." },
    { q: "State Street Bank v. Signature Financial (Fed. Cir. 1998) is known for…", a: ["The 'useful, concrete and tangible result' test", "Abolishing software patents", "Creating inter partes review"], c: 0, x: "It fueled a boom in business method patents. Bilski later repudiated the test." },
    { q: "Funk Brothers v. Kalo Inoculant (1948) held unpatentable…", a: ["A mix of naturally occurring bacterial strains", "A hybrid corn variety", "A telegraph claim"], c: 0, x: "Combining root-nodule bacteria strains that didn't inhibit each other revealed only the handiwork of nature." },
    { q: "J.E.M. Ag Supply v. Pioneer Hi-Bred (2001) held that seed-grown plants…", a: ["Can receive utility patents", "Can only receive plant patents", "Are never patentable"], c: 0, x: "The Plant Patent Act and Plant Variety Protection Act do not displace utility patent protection for plants." },
    { q: "Plant patents under the 1930 Plant Patent Act cover…", a: ["Asexually reproduced plant varieties", "Sexually reproduced seed varieties", "Genetically engineered animals"], c: 0, x: "Think grafting and cuttings. Tuber-propagated plants like the potato are excluded." },
    { q: "The first patented animal in the US was…", a: ["The Harvard OncoMouse", "Dolly the sheep", "A glow-in-the-dark fish"], c: 0, x: "Issued in 1988 to Harvard for a transgenic mouse predisposed to cancer, eight years after Chakrabarty." },
    { q: "Enfish v. Microsoft (Fed. Cir. 2016) held patent-eligible…", a: ["A self-referential database table", "An online advertising method", "A hedging scheme"], c: 0, x: "Claims directed to a specific improvement in computer functionality are not 'directed to' an abstract idea at Alice step one." },
    { q: "Brenner v. Manson (1966) held a chemical process without a known use fails…", a: ["The utility requirement", "The novelty requirement", "The best mode requirement"], c: 0, x: "'A patent is not a hunting license' — a process must have specific, substantial utility, not just research interest." },
    { q: "The AIA bars claims directed to or encompassing…", a: ["A human organism", "A transgenic mouse", "Any isolated protein"], c: 0, x: "Section 33 of the AIA codified long-standing USPTO policy against patenting humans." },
    { q: "Under the AIA, strategies for reducing tax liability are…", a: ["Deemed insufficient to distinguish prior art", "Patentable with a fee", "Reserved to the IRS"], c: 0, x: "AIA §14 effectively ended tax-strategy patents by treating them as within the prior art." },
    { q: "AIA §102(a)(1) prior art includes things patented, published, in public use, on sale, or…", a: ["Otherwise available to the public", "Known to the inventor", "Conceived by others"], c: 0, x: "That catch-all is new with the AIA. Pre-AIA §102 had no such phrase." },
    { q: "Helsinn v. Teva (2019) held that a confidential sale…", a: ["Can still trigger the AIA on-sale bar", "Is never prior art", "Only counts if made abroad"], c: 0, x: "Unanimous: Congress did not change the meaning of 'on sale' when it added 'otherwise available to the public.'" },
    { q: "Pfaff v. Wells (1998) held the on-sale bar applies when the invention is…", a: ["Subject of a commercial offer and ready for patenting", "Reduced to practice and patented", "Publicly announced at a trade show"], c: 0, x: "Pfaff sold his computer-chip socket from engineering drawings before any prototype existed — and lost his patent." },
    { q: "City of Elizabeth v. American Nicholson Pavement (1877) held a six-year road test was…", a: ["Experimental use, not public use", "A public-use bar", "Abandonment"], c: 0, x: "The inventor needed time to see how his wooden pavement held up under real traffic, so the use didn't bar his patent." },
    { q: "Egbert v. Lippmann (1881), the corset case, held that…", a: ["Even hidden use by one person can be public use", "Hidden uses never count", "Gifts are never public use"], c: 0, x: "The inventor gave corset steels to his future wife, who wore them for years before he applied." },
    { q: "Anticipation under §102 requires…", a: ["Every claim element in a single reference", "Elements spread across combined references", "Proof of commercial success"], c: 0, x: "Combining references is the province of obviousness under §103." },
    { q: "In re Hall (Fed. Cir. 1986) found a 'printed publication' in…", a: ["A single indexed thesis in a German university library", "An unpublished lab notebook", "A private email"], c: 0, x: "Public accessibility is the touchstone; one catalogued and shelved copy was enough." },
    { q: "Inherent anticipation requires that the missing feature…", a: ["Necessarily results from the prior art", "Probably results from the prior art", "Was recognized by the prior art's author"], c: 0, x: "Probabilities or possibilities don't suffice — but the skilled artisan at the time need not have recognized the inherent feature." },
    { q: "Under AIA §102(a)(2), a US patent application is prior art as of…", a: ["Its effective filing date", "Its publication date", "Its grant date"], c: 0, x: "Once published or granted, it reaches back to its effective filing date — including any properly claimed priority." },
    { q: "KSR v. Teleflex (2007) involved…", a: ["An adjustable vehicle pedal with an electronic sensor", "A pharmaceutical salt", "A plow shank clamp"], c: 0, x: "Combining known elements in a predictable way can be obvious; the Court invoked 'common sense' and the skilled artisan's creativity." },
    { q: "Graham v. John Deere (1966) involved…", a: ["A spring clamp for plow shanks", "A tractor transmission", "A combine harvester"], c: 0, x: "It set the framework: scope of prior art, differences, level of skill, and secondary considerations." },
    { q: "Which is one of the Graham factual inquiries?", a: ["Level of ordinary skill in the art", "The inventor's subjective intent", "Number of claims filed"], c: 0, x: "Also scope and content of the prior art, differences between the prior art and claims, and objective indicia of non-obviousness." },
    { q: "Secondary considerations of non-obviousness include…", a: ["Commercial success and long-felt need", "The inventor's age", "The number of claims"], c: 0, x: "Also failure of others, copying and unexpected results — but they need a nexus to the claimed features." },
    { q: "Hotchkiss v. Greenwood (1851) involved…", a: ["Clay and porcelain doorknobs", "Plow shanks", "Telephone receivers"], c: 0, x: "Swapping in a known material took only ordinary mechanical skill — an early ancestor of the obviousness doctrine." },
    { q: "§103's line that patentability 'shall not be negated by the manner' of invention rejected…", a: ["The 'flash of creative genius' test", "The utility requirement", "First-to-file"], c: 0, x: "Cuno Engineering (1941) had suggested a flash of genius was needed; the 1952 Act said painstaking research counts too." },
    { q: "Under the AIA, obviousness is judged as of…", a: ["The effective filing date", "The date of invention", "The date of grant"], c: 0, x: "Pre-AIA §103 looked to the time the invention was made." },
    { q: "Prior art that 'teaches away' from the claimed invention tends to support…", a: ["Non-obviousness", "Anticipation", "Indefiniteness"], c: 0, x: "If the art would discourage a skilled artisan from the path the inventor took, that's evidence against obviousness." },
    { q: "For a reference to be 'analogous art' it must be in the same field or…", a: ["Reasonably pertinent to the inventor's problem", "Published in English", "Less than 20 years old"], c: 0, x: "Non-analogous art can't be used in an obviousness rejection, no matter how similar it looks." },
    { q: "Patent term adjustment (PTA) compensates for…", a: ["USPTO delays during prosecution", "FDA review time", "Litigation delays"], c: 0, x: "§154(b) adds days for missed PTO deadlines and pendency beyond three years, reduced by applicant delay." },
    { q: "Patent term extension for FDA regulatory review was created by…", a: ["The Hatch-Waxman Act (1984)", "The America Invents Act", "The Bayh-Dole Act"], c: 0, x: "Codified at §156: it restores part of the patent life consumed by clinical trials and FDA approval." },
    { q: "The maximum patent term extension under §156 is…", a: ["5 years", "10 years", "2 years"], c: 0, x: "And the extended term can't run more than 14 years past FDA approval. Only one patent per product can be extended." },
    { q: "US utility patent maintenance fees fall due at…", a: ["3.5, 7.5 and 11.5 years after grant", "Every year after filing", "5, 10 and 15 years after filing"], c: 0, x: "Each has a six-month grace period with a surcharge; miss that and the patent expires." },
    { q: "A Paragraph IV certification in an ANDA asserts that a listed patent is…", a: ["Invalid or not infringed", "Expired", "Licensed to the generic"], c: 0, x: "Filing one is an artificial act of infringement under §271(e)(2), letting the brand company sue before any generic launches." },
    { q: "If the brand sues within 45 days of a Paragraph IV notice, FDA approval is stayed for up to…", a: ["30 months", "12 months", "5 years"], c: 0, x: "The 30-month stay gives time to litigate before the generic can launch." },
    { q: "The first generic to file a Paragraph IV ANDA can earn exclusivity of…", a: ["180 days", "1 year", "3 years"], c: 0, x: "The bounty for challenging brand patents — and the reason ANDAs are often filed on the first possible day." },
    { q: "The FDA publication listing patents for approved small-molecule drugs is known as…", a: ["The Orange Book", "The Purple Book", "The Green Book"], c: 0, x: "Officially 'Approved Drug Products with Therapeutic Equivalence Evaluations.' The Purple Book covers biologics." },
    { q: "Sandoz v. Amgen (2017) addressed…", a: ["The BPCIA 'patent dance'", "Antibody genus enablement", "Prosecution laches"], c: 0, x: "The biosimilar applicant's info exchange isn't enforceable by federal injunction, and notice of marketing may be given before FDA approval." },
    { q: "The §271(e)(1) safe harbor protects otherwise infringing uses…", a: ["Reasonably related to FDA submissions", "For any academic research", "For export only"], c: 0, x: "Merck v. Integra (2005) read it broadly enough to cover preclinical studies." },
    { q: "Madey v. Duke (Fed. Cir. 2002) held the common-law experimental use defense…", a: ["Is very narrow and doesn't cover a university's business", "Covers all university research", "Abolished university patents"], c: 0, x: "Research that furthers the university's legitimate business objectives isn't mere amusement or philosophical inquiry." },
    { q: "FTC v. Actavis (2013) held 'reverse payment' patent settlements are…", a: ["Subject to rule-of-reason antitrust review", "Per se illegal", "Immune from antitrust law"], c: 0, x: "Large, unexplained payments from a brand to a generic to stay off the market can violate antitrust law." },
    { q: "eBay v. MercExchange (2006) held permanent injunctions in patent cases…", a: ["Require the traditional four-factor test", "Issue automatically on a finding of infringement", "Are never available"], c: 0, x: "Irreparable injury, inadequate legal remedies, balance of hardships, public interest. The patent involved eBay's 'Buy It Now' feature." },
    { q: "Which eBay concurrence warned about firms using patents mainly to obtain licensing fees?", a: ["Justice Kennedy's", "Justice Scalia's", "Justice Thomas's"], c: 0, x: "He noted injunctions can be used as bargaining tools to charge exorbitant fees — an early judicial nod to the NPE debate." },
    { q: "The Georgia-Pacific factors are used to determine…", a: ["A reasonable royalty", "Claim construction", "Obviousness"], c: 0, x: "Fifteen factors from a 1970 S.D.N.Y. decision, framing a hypothetical negotiation between the parties." },
    { q: "The Panduit factors are used to prove…", a: ["Lost profits", "A reasonable royalty", "Willfulness"], c: 0, x: "Demand, absence of acceptable non-infringing substitutes, manufacturing and marketing capacity, and the amount of profit." },
    { q: "Samsung v. Apple (2016) clarified §289's 'article of manufacture' for design damages can be…", a: ["A component, not necessarily the whole product", "Only the whole product", "Only the packaging"], c: 0, x: "The first Supreme Court design patent case in over a century; the jury had awarded Samsung's entire profit on infringing phones." },
    { q: "§289 lets a design patent owner recover…", a: ["The infringer's total profit on the article", "Only a reasonable royalty", "Automatic treble damages"], c: 0, x: "A remedy unique to design patents, dating back to the 1887 'carpet cases.'" },
    { q: "WesternGeco v. ION (2018) allowed a patentee to recover…", a: ["Foreign lost profits tied to §271(f)(2) infringement", "Punitive damages", "Fees in every case"], c: 0, x: "Because the domestic act of supplying components was the infringement, lost foreign survey contracts were recoverable." },
    { q: "Commil v. Cisco (2015) held that a good-faith belief in a patent's invalidity…", a: ["Is no defense to induced infringement", "Is a complete defense", "Halves the damages"], c: 0, x: "Belief in non-infringement can negate the intent for inducement, but belief in invalidity cannot." },
    { q: "Global-Tech v. SEB (2011) held the knowledge needed for inducement can be shown by…", a: ["Willful blindness", "Mere negligence", "Constructive notice"], c: 0, x: "SEB's competitor copied its deep fryer and deliberately avoided telling its patent attorney that it was a copy." },
    { q: "Limelight v. Akamai (2014) held there is no inducement liability without…", a: ["Underlying direct infringement", "A written contract", "A foreign sale"], c: 0, x: "On remand, the Federal Circuit broadened when one actor is responsible for steps performed by others." },
    { q: "Contributory infringement under §271(c) requires that the component…", a: ["Not be a staple article with substantial non-infringing use", "Be separately patented", "Be imported"], c: 0, x: "Sell a component especially adapted for infringement, knowing it, and you can be liable as a contributory infringer." },
    { q: "The patent 'first-sale doctrine' is better known as…", a: ["Patent exhaustion", "Laches", "Patent misuse"], c: 0, x: "An authorized sale ends the patentee's control over that particular item — use and resale are free." },
    { q: "Impression Products v. Lexmark (2017) involved…", a: ["Refurbished printer toner cartridges", "Patented soybean seeds", "Smartphone chips"], c: 0, x: "Authorized sales exhaust US patent rights even when made abroad, and post-sale restrictions can't be enforced through patent law." },
    { q: "Bowman v. Monsanto (2013) held exhaustion does not let a farmer…", a: ["Plant patented seeds to grow new ones", "Sell the harvest as feed", "Resell seeds he bought"], c: 0, x: "Planting commodity soybeans and saving the progeny made new copies of the patented invention." },
    { q: "Quanta v. LG Electronics (2008) held exhaustion applies to…", a: ["Method patents substantially embodied in sold items", "Only apparatus claims", "Nothing that is licensed"], c: 0, x: "Intel's authorized sales of chips exhausted LG's method patents that the chips substantially embodied." },
    { q: "Deepsouth Packing v. Laitram (1972) led Congress to enact…", a: ["§271(f) on supplying components for assembly abroad", "§101", "The PTAB"], c: 0, x: "Deepsouth shipped unassembled shrimp deveiners abroad. Congress closed the loophole in 1984." },
    { q: "Microsoft v. AT&T (2007) held that copies of Windows made abroad from a US master disk…", a: ["Weren't 'supplied from' the US under §271(f)", "Infringed under §271(f)", "Were exhausted"], c: 0, x: "Software in the abstract isn't a combinable component, and the foreign-made copies didn't leave the US." },
    { q: "Life Technologies v. Promega (2017) held a single commodity component…", a: ["Is not a 'substantial portion' under §271(f)(1)", "Always triggers §271(f) liability", "Is exhausted on export"], c: 0, x: "Life Tech shipped only the Taq polymerase to its UK plant; one component can't be a substantial portion of the components." },
    { q: "Importing into the US a product made abroad by a US-patented process infringes under…", a: ["§271(g)", "§102(a)", "§156"], c: 0, x: "Added in 1988 to stop manufacturers from moving patented processes offshore and shipping the product back." },
    { q: "TC Heartland v. Kraft (2017) held a domestic corporation 'resides' for patent venue only…", a: ["In its state of incorporation", "Wherever it sells products", "Where its CEO lives"], c: 0, x: "The decision drained the Eastern District of Texas of much of its patent docket." },
    { q: "The patent venue statute is…", a: ["28 U.S.C. §1400(b)", "35 U.S.C. §1400", "28 U.S.C. §1331"], c: 0, x: "Venue lies where the defendant resides or where it infringed and has a regular and established place of business." },
    { q: "Exclusive appellate jurisdiction over patent cases belongs to…", a: ["The Federal Circuit", "The Ninth Circuit", "The D.C. Circuit"], c: 0, x: "Created in 1982, merging the Court of Customs and Patent Appeals with the appellate division of the Court of Claims." },
    { q: "Gunn v. Minton (2013) held legal malpractice claims arising from patent cases…", a: ["Generally belong in state court", "Must go to the Federal Circuit", "Go to the PTAB"], c: 0, x: "A state-law claim that happens to involve a hypothetical patent question doesn't 'arise under' federal patent law." },
    { q: "The ITC can block infringing imports under…", a: ["Section 337 of the Tariff Act of 1930", "35 U.S.C. §101", "The Lanham Act"], c: 0, x: "Its remedy is an exclusion order enforced by Customs — no money damages, but often faster than district court." },
    { q: "Festo v. Shoketsu (2002) concerned…", a: ["Prosecution history estoppel and equivalents", "Patent term adjustment", "Design patent damages"], c: 0, x: "A narrowing amendment presumptively surrenders equivalents, rebuttable if unforeseeable, tangential or otherwise not reasonably describable." },
    { q: "Warner-Jenkinson v. Hilton Davis (1997) held the doctrine of equivalents applies…", a: ["Element by element", "To the invention as a whole", "Only to method claims"], c: 0, x: "Applying it to the invention as a whole would let it swallow the claims' notice function." },
    { q: "Graver Tank v. Linde Air (1950) is known for the equivalence test of…", a: ["Function, way, result", "Teaching, suggestion, motivation", "Machine or transformation"], c: 0, x: "Does the substitute perform substantially the same function in substantially the same way to achieve the same result?" },
    { q: "The 'all-elements rule' requires that…", a: ["Every claim limitation be met literally or by an equivalent", "Any one element be present", "Only independent claims be considered"], c: 0, x: "If an equivalent would read a limitation entirely out of the claim, it is 'vitiated' and the doctrine of equivalents fails." },
    { q: "Under Johnson & Johnston (2002), subject matter disclosed but not claimed is…", a: ["Dedicated to the public for equivalents purposes", "Automatically claimed", "Reserved to the patentee"], c: 0, x: "You can't recapture through the doctrine of equivalents what you disclosed but chose not to claim." },
    { q: "Therasense v. Becton Dickinson (2011, en banc) requires inequitable conduct to show…", a: ["But-for materiality and specific intent to deceive", "Mere negligence", "Any omission"], c: 0, x: "The court called inequitable conduct an 'absolute plague' and tightened both prongs." },
    { q: "If proven, inequitable conduct renders…", a: ["The entire patent unenforceable", "Only the affected claim invalid", "The examiner liable"], c: 0, x: "The 'atomic bomb' of patent law — it can also infect related patents in the family." },
    { q: "The duty of candor before the USPTO is codified in…", a: ["37 C.F.R. §1.56", "35 U.S.C. §101", "37 C.F.R. §1.8"], c: 0, x: "Everyone substantively involved in prosecution must disclose information known to be material to patentability." },
    { q: "An Information Disclosure Statement (IDS) is used to…", a: ["Cite known material prior art to the examiner", "Pay issue fees", "Name the inventors"], c: 0, x: "Filed under 37 C.F.R. §§1.97–1.98, it's how applicants satisfy the duty of disclosure." },
    { q: "Supplemental examination, created by the AIA, lets a patent owner…", a: ["Have the PTO consider new information to cure inequitable-conduct risk", "Extend the patent term", "Add new matter to claims"], c: 0, x: "Information considered in supplemental examination generally can't later support an unenforceability charge." },
    { q: "A Request for Continued Examination (RCE) is typically filed…", a: ["After a final rejection to keep prosecuting", "Before the first filing", "To appeal to the Federal Circuit"], c: 0, x: "It reopens prosecution of the same application with a fee — no new application needed." },
    { q: "A continuation-in-part (CIP) application…", a: ["Adds new matter to the parent's disclosure", "Only presents new claims on the same disclosure", "Only changes inventorship"], c: 0, x: "Claims relying on the new matter get only the CIP's filing date." },
    { q: "A divisional application usually follows…", a: ["A restriction requirement", "A final rejection", "A notice of allowance"], c: 0, x: "§121 shields divisionals from double patenting rejections based on the parent." },
    { q: "To claim priority under §120, a continuation must be filed while…", a: ["The parent is still pending", "The parent is expired", "Within 5 years after grant"], c: 0, x: "Copendency is essential — once the parent issues or is abandoned, the chain is broken." },
    { q: "Priority to a foreign application under the Paris Convention is claimed under…", a: ["§119(a)", "§112(b)", "§271(g)"], c: 0, x: "Domestic benefit to a provisional is §119(e); to a nonprovisional, §120; PCT links run through §365." },
    { q: "Amendments may not add new matter under…", a: ["§132", "§101", "§156"], c: 0, x: "Support must exist in the application as originally filed — the bedrock rule of prosecution." },
    { q: "A §145 civil action against the USPTO Director is now filed in…", a: ["The Eastern District of Virginia", "Any district court", "The Court of Federal Claims"], c: 0, x: "The AIA moved it from D.C. to Alexandria's home court. Unlike a direct appeal, new evidence may be introduced." },
    { q: "Peter v. NantKwest (2019) held §145's 'all the expenses' provision does not include…", a: ["The PTO's attorney fees", "Printing costs", "Expert witness fees"], c: 0, x: "The American Rule's presumption against fee-shifting applied; 'expenses' isn't specific enough to cover salaried PTO lawyers." },
    { q: "Track One prioritized examination aims for final disposition within about…", a: ["12 months", "3 months", "36 months"], c: 0, x: "For an extra fee and with claim limits (4 independent, 30 total), the application jumps the queue." },
    { q: "Since December 2022, micro entity status reduces most USPTO fees by…", a: ["80%", "50%", "25%"], c: 0, x: "The Unleashing American Innovators Act raised the micro entity discount from 75% to 80%." },
    { q: "Since December 2022, small entity status reduces most USPTO fees by…", a: ["60%", "80%", "10%"], c: 0, x: "The discount was 50% for years; the Unleashing American Innovators Act raised it to 60%." },
    { q: "A restriction requirement issues when an application claims…", a: ["Independent or distinct inventions", "Indefinite subject matter", "Too few claims"], c: 0, x: "The applicant must elect one invention; the others can be pursued in divisional applications." },
    { q: "Ex parte reexamination is ordered when a request raises…", a: ["A substantial new question of patentability", "A reasonable likelihood of prevailing", "A pending lawsuit"], c: 0, x: "IPR, by contrast, requires a reasonable likelihood that the petitioner will prevail on at least one claim." },
    { q: "Inter partes review replaced inter partes reexamination in…", a: ["September 2012", "January 1999", "November 2018"], c: 0, x: "IPR took effect September 16, 2012, one year after the AIA was signed." },
    { q: "A reissue application that broadens the claims must be filed within…", a: ["2 years of grant", "1 year of grant", "Any time before expiration"], c: 0, x: "§251. Narrowing reissues have no such deadline, but the error must be one made without deceptive intent." },
    { q: "Intervening rights protect third parties when claims are…", a: ["Substantively changed in reissue or reexamination", "Licensed to competitors", "Allowed to expire"], c: 0, x: "Someone who began using the invention before the amended claims issued may be shielded from liability." },
    { q: "The USPTO's examining guide is called the…", a: ["MPEP", "TMEP", "CPR"], c: 0, x: "The Manual of Patent Examining Procedure. The TMEP is its trademark counterpart." },
    { q: "The transitional phrase 'comprising' is…", a: ["Open-ended", "Closed", "Limited to the preferred embodiment"], c: 0, x: "It means 'including but not limited to' — additional unrecited elements don't avoid infringement." },
    { q: "'Consisting essentially of' excludes elements that…", a: ["Materially affect the basic and novel characteristics", "Are listed in the claim", "Appear in the spec"], c: 0, x: "A middle ground between open 'comprising' and closed 'consisting of.'" },
    { q: "A Jepson claim…", a: ["Recites the prior art and then 'the improvement comprising'", "Defines a product by its process", "Covers only ornamental designs"], c: 0, x: "Named after Ex parte Jepson (1917). The preamble is treated as an admission of prior art." },
    { q: "A Markush group recites alternatives in the form…", a: ["'selected from the group consisting of'", "'means for' plus function", "'the improvement comprising'"], c: 0, x: "Named for chemist Eugene Markush, whose dye patent popularized the format; ubiquitous in chemical claims." },
    { q: "Abbott v. Sandoz (Fed. Cir. 2009, en banc) held product-by-process claims are infringed only…", a: ["By products made by the recited process", "By any identical product", "By the process alone"], c: 0, x: "For infringement purposes, the process steps are limitations — whatever the claim's validity analysis." },
    { q: "A 'Beauregard claim' covers…", a: ["A computer-readable medium storing instructions", "A new plant variety", "An ornamental design"], c: 0, x: "Named after In re Beauregard (1995), where the PTO conceded such claims were patentable subject matter." },
    { q: "US excess claims fees kick in above…", a: ["3 independent or 20 total claims", "10 independent or 50 total", "1 independent or 5 total"], c: 0, x: "Multiple dependent claims carry their own fee too." },
    { q: "The statutory maximum period to respond to an Office Action is…", a: ["6 months", "3 months", "1 year"], c: 0, x: "§133. The usual shortened period is three months, extendable by fee up to the six-month limit." },
    { q: "Under the AIA, derivation proceedings replaced…", a: ["Interference proceedings", "Ex parte reexamination", "Reissue applications"], c: 0, x: "Interferences asked who invented first; derivation asks only whether an earlier filer took the invention from the other." },
    { q: "Since the AIA, a patent application may be filed by…", a: ["An assignee as the applicant", "Only the inventors", "Only a registered attorney"], c: 0, x: "§118 allows assignees and parties with sufficient proprietary interest to apply in their own name." },
