    { q: "Bayh-Dole 'march-in rights' let the government…", a: ["Require licensing of a funded invention in certain cases", "Block university exports", "Seize any patent"], c: 0, x: "Despite many petitions over the decades, the government has never actually exercised march-in rights." },
    { q: "When the US government uses a patented invention, the owner's remedy is under…", a: ["28 U.S.C. §1498", "35 U.S.C. §271(a)", "35 U.S.C. §101"], c: 0, x: "Reasonable and entire compensation in the Court of Federal Claims — but no injunction." },
    { q: "Secrecy orders under the Invention Secrecy Act are imposed when publication…", a: ["Would harm national security", "Is requested by the inventor", "Would reveal trade secrets"], c: 0, x: "Thousands remain in effect. Violating one can forfeit the patent and lead to penalties." },
    { q: "Absent a foreign filing license, a US-made invention may be filed abroad once the US application has been pending…", a: ["6 months", "30 days", "2 years"], c: 0, x: "§184. In practice the filing receipt usually grants a license; filing abroad without one can bar a US patent." },
    { q: "Egyptian Goddess v. Swisa (2008, en banc) adopted for design infringement…", a: ["The ordinary observer test", "The point-of-novelty test", "The function-way-result test"], c: 0, x: "It abandoned the separate point-of-novelty test. The design was for a nail buffer." },
