    { s: "Why did the invention-disclosure form feel underloved?", p: "Engineers hate paperwork. Patents love it." },
    { s: "What did the inventor say when asked about 'first commercial use'?", p: "'What's a first commercial use?' the litigator sighed." },
    { s: "Why did the on-sale bar terrify startups?", p: "One quote, one pitch deck, one demo — clock starts." },
    { s: "What's the experimental-use exception?", p: "The narrow rescue for inventors who put a prototype in the wild." },
    { s: "Why did the AIA one-year grace period reassure inventors?", p: "'I published, then filed within a year — I'm okay in the US.' (Everywhere else? No.)" },
    { s: "What did the foreign filing license say?", p: "\"Don't publish abroad first. Or ask nicely.\"" },
    { s: "Why did the export-control rules complicate technology transfer?", p: "Some know-how is deemed 'exported' the moment a foreign national reads it." },
    { s: "What did the joint-development agreement do?", p: "Decide who owned what before anyone invented it." },
    { s: "Why did the background/foreground IP distinction matter?", p: "Background is what you brought. Foreground is what you invented together." },
    { s: "What's an implied license?", p: "The one you didn't sign — and might still owe." },
    { s: "Why did patent exhaustion feel absolute post-Lexmark?", p: "Sold once, no infringement claim on that unit ever again — even abroad." },
    { s: "What's the first-sale doctrine's cousin in patents?", p: "Also called exhaustion. Same energy." },
    { s: "Why did the patent misuse defense feel obsolete?", p: "Post-Illinois Tool Works, tying without market power isn't misuse." },
    { s: "What's an inequitable conduct defense?", p: "The nuclear option — often threatened, rarely won." },
    { s: "Why did Therasense narrow inequitable conduct?", p: "\"But-for materiality AND specific intent to deceive.\" The bar went up." },
    { s: "What's the duty of candor?", p: "Everyone involved with prosecution owes it." },
    { s: "Why did the IDS submission grow into a monster?", p: "'When in doubt, cite it.' 400-reference IDS submissions ensued." },
    { s: "What's a supplemental examination?", p: "The AIA's way of letting patentees fix pre-grant candor issues without penalty." },
    { s: "Why did the reissue application feel awkward?", p: "You admitted your patent was defective." },
    { s: "What's a broadening reissue's deadline?", p: "Two years from grant. Miss it and the claims are frozen." },
    { s: "Why did the patent term adjustment feel like a bonus?", p: "PTO delays don't cost you calendar time — they extend your monopoly." },
    { s: "What did the PTA calculation look like?", p: "A spreadsheet from the darkest place in your soul." },
    { s: "Why did patent term extension for pharma feel political?", p: "Five extra years, capped at 14 total — brand vs. generic in one clause." },
    { s: "What's the Hatch-Waxman ANDA process?", p: "A framework where copying is fast and litigation is scheduled." },
    { s: "Why did the Paragraph IV certification start a war?", p: "It announced 'your patent is invalid or not infringed.'" },
    { s: "What's a 30-month stay?", p: "The pause that funds pharma litigation." },
    { s: "Why did BPCIA look like Hatch-Waxman for biologics?", p: "It was — with more procedural complexity and larger stakes." },
    { s: "What did the 'patent dance' involve?", p: "A statutorily-choreographed information exchange nobody enjoys." },
    { s: "Why did biosimilars trail biologics by years?", p: "Regulatory + patent thickets + clinical trials." },
    { s: "What's Orange Book listing?", p: "Registering your patents with FDA. Makes ANDA challenges the reference point." },
    { s: "Why did Purple Book matter for biologics?", p: "The parallel listing. Different chapter, same drama." },
    { s: "What's evergreening?", p: "A pharma marketing term. Its use as a technical term is contested." },
    { s: "Why did the SPC in Europe echo PTE?", p: "Same idea. Different name. Different quirks." },
    { s: "What did the unified patent court promise?", p: "One decision across most of the EU — for better and worse." },
    { s: "Why did some patentees opt out of UPC?", p: "One court that can revoke you EVERYWHERE at once." },
    { s: "What's the Boards of Appeal at the EPO?", p: "Where European patents go to be tested — and often survive." },
    { s: "Why did the added-matter objection loom large at the EPO?", p: "Cannot add subject matter beyond the application as filed. Ever." },
    { s: "What did the German bifurcated system split?", p: "Infringement (fast) from validity (slower). The 'injunction gap' was born." },
    { s: "Why did China become a patent powerhouse?", p: "One million-plus applications a year and rising." },
    { s: "What's China's utility model?", p: "A shorter, cheaper, unexamined patent. Formidable in enforcement." },
    { s: "Why did design patents get suddenly cool?", p: "Apple v. Samsung and $539 million." },
    { s: "What did the Federal Circuit fight about in Apple v. Samsung?", p: "'Article of manufacture' — the whole product, or just the part with the design?" },
    { s: "Why did the Supreme Court remand Apple v. Samsung?", p: "To let the trial court answer that question." },
    { s: "What's a §102 rejection?", p: "'Somebody did this already.' Full stop." },
    { s: "Why did the anticipation standard feel absolute?", p: "One reference. Every element. As arranged. Full stop." },
    { s: "What's an obviousness rejection?", p: "'Two references, a POSITA, and reasonable motivation.'" },
    { s: "Why did TSM tests fade after KSR?", p: "Rigid formulas out; common sense in." },
    { s: "What's a teaching-away argument?", p: "Rare, powerful, hard to make." },
    { s: "Why did secondary considerations feel underused?", p: "Commercial success, long-felt need, unexpected results — undervalued by drafters." },
    { s: "What did the Graham factors survive?", p: "Sixty years and counting." },
    { s: "Why did the prior-art declaration matter?", p: "Sworn testimony beats attorney argument at the PTO." },
    { s: "What's a Rule 131 declaration?", p: "'I invented this before your reference' — pre-AIA nostalgia." },
    { s: "Why did the AIA kill 131 for most applications?", p: "First-inventor-to-file. Filing date is the date." },
    { s: "What's a 132 declaration?", p: "The evidence you attach to a response — useful in almost every art unit." },
    { s: "Why did the examiner interview save your case?", p: "Sometimes twenty minutes of talking saves twenty months of back-and-forth." },
    { s: "What's an AFCP 2.0 request?", p: "The examiner-encouraged short amendment after final. Free three hours." },
    { s: "Why did every attorney learn the phrase 'consistent with the specification'?", p: "Because that's how you construe claims your way." },
    { s: "What did claim differentiation warn against?", p: "'Two claims that mean the same thing are drafted badly.'" },
    { s: "Why did dependent claims matter more than they looked?", p: "Fallback positions. Also, claim-differentiation ammunition." },
    { s: "What did jepson claims signal?", p: "'The stuff before whereby is prior art. The stuff after is new.'" },
    { s: "Why did jepson-style claims fade in the US?", p: "Voluntary confession that goes on the record." },
    { s: "What's a beauregard claim?", p: "A CRM claim. Named after the case that blessed it." },
    { s: "Why did software claims retreat post-Alice?", p: "System, method, CRM — no shape saved the abstract idea." },
    { s: "What survives Alice for software?", p: "Specific technical improvements. Concrete performance gains. Never 'organize information.'" },
    { s: "Why did Enfish give practitioners hope?", p: "A claim can be about improving computer functionality — that's not abstract." },
    { s: "What did Berkheimer teach us?", p: "Alice step two has fact questions. Summary judgment isn't automatic." },
    { s: "Why did Chamberlain shrink garage-door openers?", p: "'Wireless communication' was too broad — abstract on the facts." },
    { s: "What's the 2019 PEG?", p: "The USPTO's Alice framework: identify the abstract idea, look for practical application." },
    { s: "Why did the 2024 update on AI matter?", p: "Guidance on how to claim AI without being reflexively abstract." },
    { s: "What's an inventive concept?", p: "The 'something more' at Alice step two." },
    { s: "Why did routine and conventional feel damning?", p: "If the extra is routine, there's no inventive concept." },
    { s: "What did BASCOM survive on?", p: "Non-conventional ordering of conventional components." },
    { s: "Why did DDR Holdings win at §101?", p: "It solved a problem rooted in computer networks — not an idea humans had for years." },
    { s: "What's a Rule 11 sanction for a bad patent case?", p: "Rare. But increasing." },
    { s: "Why did the exceptional-case fee award finally get used?", p: "Post-Octane Fitness, judges have more discretion." },
    { s: "What's the §285 attorney-fee award standard?", p: "'Exceptional' — post-Octane, that's flexible." },
    { s: "Why did venue transfers accelerate after Volkswagen?", p: "The Fifth Circuit's convenience factors got teeth." },
    { s: "What's a §273 defense?", p: "Prior commercial use — a partial shield against surprise patent enforcement." },
    { s: "Why did the AIA add prior-user rights broadly?", p: "Because first-inventor-to-file punishes trade-secret keepers." },
    { s: "What's a walker process claim?", p: "Antitrust liability for enforcing a fraudulently-procured patent." },
    { s: "Why did sham-litigation claims rarely succeed?", p: "The bar is intentionally high. Petition-immunity favors patentees." },
    { s: "What's Noerr-Pennington?", p: "Immunity for petitioning the government — including filing patent suits." },
    { s: "Why did SEP FRAND litigation grow?", p: "Standards essential patents + mobile phones + billions of dollars." },
    { s: "What's an anti-suit injunction in SEP wars?", p: "Court A telling you not to litigate the same patents in Court B." },
    { s: "Why did the anti-anti-suit injunction exist?", p: "Because Court B fights back." },
    { s: "What did the Sisvel v. Haier ruling out of Germany say?", p: "SEP licensing has procedural requirements the implementer must meet." },
    { s: "Why did FRAND rates litigation become forensic?", p: "Comparable licenses, top-down, bottom-up — every methodology on the table." },
    { s: "What's an unwilling licensee?", p: "The buzzword that decides who gets an injunction under FRAND." },
    { s: "Why did the EU push for SEP transparency?", p: "Because rate-setting behind closed doors invites regulation." },
    { s: "What's an EU regulation on SEPs?", p: "The 2023 proposal that patentees hated and implementers loved." },
    { s: "Why did patent pools thrive in standards markets?", p: "One license, many patents. Efficient. Politically contested." },
    { s: "What's MPEG LA?", p: "The patent pool that showed the model. Video-codec licensing at scale." },
    { s: "Why did the Nortel patent auction of 2011 shake the industry?", p: "$4.5 billion. Rockstar consortium vs. Google. Smartphone wars were on." },
    { s: "What's a patent portfolio's 'net-neutral' license?", p: "A cross-license that lets both parties operate — money changes hands, or doesn't." },
    { s: "Why did Alice cause patent transactions to reprice?", p: "Software portfolios lost value. Hardware held up." },
    { s: "What did patent monetization look like post-Alice?", p: "Smaller assertions. Careful curation. Longer discovery." },
    { s: "Why did trade dress claims tag along?", p: "Sometimes look-and-feel outlives the utility patent." },
    { s: "What's a §271(g) claim?", p: "Import a product made abroad by a US-patented process — you infringe." },
    { s: "Why did §271(f) matter overseas?", p: "Shipping components from the US to assemble abroad triggered infringement." },
    { s: "What did Life Techs v. Promega narrow?", p: "'Substantial portion' means quantitative — one component isn't enough." },
    { s: "Why did Deepsouth v. Laitram spawn §271(f)?", p: "The Court refused to reach exports. Congress reached for them anyway." },
    { s: "What's a divided infringement?", p: "Multiple actors performing one method — attribution rules decide who's liable." },
    { s: "Why did Akamai v. Limelight clean up divided infringement?", p: "One entity liable when it directs or controls others' performance." },
    { s: "What's induced infringement?", p: "'You told them to do it, knowing what they'd do.'" },
    { s: "Why did Global-Tech raise the mens rea bar?", p: "Willful blindness counts. Negligence doesn't." },
    { s: "What did Commil narrow further?", p: "'Good-faith belief of invalidity' isn't a defense to induced infringement." },
    { s: "Why did Halo v. Pulse make willfulness easier?", p: "Discretionary standard. No more In re Seagate rigidity." },
    { s: "What's an opinion of counsel worth?", p: "Post-Seagate less than before. Post-Halo maybe more again." },
    { s: "Why did patent malpractice cases stay complex?", p: "Case-within-a-case: prove the underlying patent case should have won." },
    { s: "What did the Federal Circuit clarify about malpractice jurisdiction?", p: "Post-Gunn v. Minton, state courts handle most claims — Fed Cir doesn't touch them." },
    { s: "Why did design-around become an engineering discipline?", p: "Because valid patents mean you route around them." },
    { s: "What's a freedom-to-operate analysis?", p: "The scariest memo any inventor asks for." },
    { s: "Why did the FTO opinion feel forever?", p: "Every claim, every product, every geography — the matrix explodes." },
    { s: "What did in-house counsel prefer over formal opinions?", p: "Detailed clearance searches with attorney summaries — less exposure, more coverage." },
    { s: "Why did trade secrets return to fashion?", p: "Post-Alice, some inventions had nowhere better to go." },
    { s: "What's the Defend Trade Secrets Act?", p: "Federal cause of action, 2016. Trade secrets went national." },
    { s: "Why did the DTSA include ex parte seizure?", p: "For flight risks with trade-secret files on a laptop." },
    { s: "What did the whistleblower immunity clause do?", p: "Protect employees who disclose trade secrets to lawyers or the government in confidence." },
    { s: "Why did every NDA suddenly cite 18 U.S.C. § 1833(b)?", p: "Because failing to notify employees limits your DTSA remedies." },
    { s: "What's a covenant not to compete?", p: "Sometimes trade-secret protection. Sometimes labor-mobility restraint. State law decides which." },
    { s: "Why did the FTC try to ban non-competes?", p: "Because it thought most were unnecessary and anticompetitive." },
    { s: "What survived after the FTC ban was enjoined?", p: "State-by-state law. California vs. Florida. Same words, opposite outcomes." },
    { s: "What did the DOJ v. patents look like in 2024?", p: "Standard-essential patents, FRAND rate cases, and the occasional antitrust warning shot." },
    { s: "Why did SEP holders and standards implementers stop trusting each other?", p: "Because 'reasonable' was defined by whoever spoke last." },
    { s: "What did the Federal Circuit tell everyone in 2024?", p: "'You keep petitioning us to fix the same three doctrines. We're not going to.'" },
    { s: "Why did AI-generated inventions raise §115 questions?", p: "Because 'inventor' means a natural person — and the USPTO said so." },
    { s: "What's the Thaler v. Vidal takeaway?", p: "'DABUS may have invented it, but only a human can be named on the application.'" },
    { s: "Why did AI-assisted invention guidance take so long?", p: "Because 'significantly contributed' is subjective at every layer." },
    { s: "What did the USPTO's 2024 AI guidance require?", p: "A natural person had to make a significant contribution to each claim. Prompts alone aren't enough." },
    { s: "Why did the joint-inventorship analysis get harder with AI?", p: "Because your prompt engineer might have contributed more than the model. Or less. Nobody's sure." },
    { s: "What did the biggest patent law firms do in 2024?", p: "Started AI-invention practice groups. Not sure what to charge for them yet." },
    { s: "How many patent attorneys does it take to change a light bulb?", p: "At least one. We'd rather claim 'one or more' to be safe." },
    { s: "What's a patent attorney's favorite number?", p: "\"At least one.\"" },
    { s: "What do you call a claim with 47 limitations?", p: "Valid. And infringed by absolutely no one." },
    { s: "What do you call a claim with three limitations?", p: "Broad, beautiful and anticipated by a 1962 trade journal." },
    { s: "What did the provisional say on its first birthday?", p: "\"Make a wish. Ideally a nonprovisional.\"" },
    { s: "What's the difference between a patent attorney and a magician?", p: "The magician makes things disappear. We can only make claims narrower." },
    { s: "Why did the examiner cite a cave painting?", p: "It disclosed a round, rolling element. Inherently." },
    { s: "What's an inventor's favorite phrase?", p: "\"It's just like X, but better.\" The examiner's reply: \"So, it's X.\"" },
    { s: "Why was the patent drawing so calm?", p: "It knew every part had a reference numeral." },
    { s: "What did reference numeral 12 say to reference numeral 14?", p: "\"Where's 13?\" Nobody ever knows." },
    { s: "Why did FIG. 7 feel left out?", p: "The spec mentioned it once, briefly, as 'an alternative embodiment.'" },
    { s: "Why are patent attorneys great at parties?", p: "They can talk about a toaster for 40 pages." },
    { s: "What's a toaster, in patent-speak?", p: "A thermal bread-modification apparatus comprising at least one heating element." },
    { s: "Why did the claim go on a diet?", p: "The examiner said it was too broad." },
    { s: "Why did 'consisting of' have no friends?", p: "It was closed to everyone else." },
    { s: "What did 'consisting essentially of' say at the party?", p: "\"New members welcome, as long as they don't change anything material.\"" },
    { s: "What did antecedent basis say on a first date?", p: "\"Before we get to 'the,' can we start with 'a'?\"" },
    { s: "Why was the means-plus-function claim so anxious?", p: "Everything depended on what the spec said about structure." },
    { s: "What did §112(f) say to the word 'module'?", p: "\"Nice try. You're a nonce word.\"" },
    { s: "What's a patent attorney's idea of a thriller?", p: "A 30-day deadline and a missing inventor signature." },
    { s: "What do you call an inventor who can't be found to sign the declaration?", p: "A substitute statement." },
    { s: "Why did the engineer start signing every page of the lab notebook?", p: "Witnesses, dates and a deep, lingering fear of interference proceedings." },
    { s: "When is an invention conceived?", p: "When it's definite and permanent in the inventor's mind. Usually around 2 a.m." },
    { s: "What's reduction to practice?", p: "The moment the napkin sketch actually works." },
    { s: "Why did the napkin sketch get excited?", p: "It heard it might become Exhibit A." },
    { s: "What's an examiner's favorite reference?", p: "The one that shows your exact claim, published a week before your priority date." },
    { s: "What's the most hopeful sentence in an office action?", p: "\"Claim 7 would be allowable if rewritten in independent form.\"" },
    { s: "What's an examiner's amendment?", p: "A two-minute phone call that saves four months. Always answer the phone." },
    { s: "Why do patent attorneys love the Notice of Allowance?", p: "It's the only USPTO letter that doesn't ask for an argument." },
    { s: "What's the issue fee?", p: "The cover charge to leave a party you've been trying to leave for four years." },
    { s: "Why did the patent stop celebrating its grant day?", p: "It remembered the maintenance fees." },
    { s: "What do you call a patent that missed its maintenance fee?", p: "Public domain, with regret." },
    { s: "What's the most-used word in a petition to revive?", p: "\"Unintentional.\"" },
    { s: "Why did the patent family need a bigger table at the holidays?", p: "Two continuations, a divisional and a CIP nobody fully understands." },
    { s: "What's a terminal disclaimer?", p: "The prenup a continuation signs so it never outlives its parent." },
    { s: "What's obviousness-type double patenting?", p: "Getting rejected over your own earlier patent. The ultimate self-own." },
    { s: "Why did the examiner cite the applicant's own brochure?", p: "Sometimes the best prior art comes from your own marketing team." },
    { s: "What's patent counsel's least favorite email subject line?", p: "\"Quick question about our booth demo.\"" },
    { s: "What's a provisional application's biggest weakness?", p: "It's only as good as what you actually wrote in it." },
    { s: "Why did the provisional application consist of 14 slides?", p: "Someone had a pitch meeting the next morning." },
    { s: "What's the best part of a provisional?", p: "No claims required. The worst part? No claims required." },
    { s: "What happens at 30 months in a PCT application?", p: "National phase. Or, as the budget calls it, 'the reckoning.'" },
    { s: "What did the international written opinion say?", p: "\"Novelty: no. Inventive step: no. Have a great day.\"" },
    { s: "Why did the European examiner keep asking about the problem?", p: "The problem-solution approach needs a problem. Ideally an objective one." },
    { s: "What's the scariest party invitation in Europe?", p: "A notice of opposition, filed by your closest competitor." },
    { s: "Why did the trademark feel superior to the patent?", p: "It can live forever, as long as it keeps working." },
    { s: "What did the trademark say to the patent at its retirement party?", p: "\"Twenty years? I'm just getting started.\"" },
    { s: "Why was the generic mark so sad?", p: "Everyone used its name, and nobody remembered it was a brand." },
    { s: "What's a trademark attorney's nightmare?", p: "Hearing the brand used as a verb in the board meeting." },
    { s: "Why did the descriptive mark join a gym?", p: "It had to build secondary meaning." },
    { s: "What did the specimen promise the examining attorney?", p: "\"I'm a real product label, not a mockup. Honest.\"" },
    { s: "Why did the opposition proceeding take two years?", p: "Discovery on whether 'Zesty' and 'Zestee' sound the same." },
    { s: "What's the trademark motto?", p: "Use it or lose it. Abandonment is not a strategy." },
    { s: "Why does a copyright never fill out paperwork?", p: "It exists the moment you fix it in a tangible medium. Very low maintenance." },
    { s: "What did the copyright say to the patent?", p: "\"You needed an examiner? I just needed a pen.\"" },
    { s: "What did the trade secret say at the IP family reunion?", p: "Nothing. That's the whole point." },
    { s: "Why did the trade secret refuse to file a patent application?", p: "\"Publication? After everything we've been through?\"" },
    { s: "What's a clean room?", p: "Where engineers rebuild something without ever having seen it. Allegedly." },
    { s: "Why did the reverse engineer sleep soundly?", p: "Taking the product apart is fair. Taking the blueprints isn't." },
    { s: "Why did the examiner find Boolean search romantic?", p: "Nothing says love like 'widget AND (rotat* OR spin*) NOT toy.'" },
    { s: "When does a prior art search find the perfect reference?", p: "The day after the response is filed." },
    { s: "Why did the searcher drive to the library?", p: "The best prior art was in a 1974 conference proceeding nobody ever digitized." },
    { s: "Why did the conference poster become famous?", p: "It was cited as prior art in four separate office actions." },
    { s: "What did the tech transfer office say to the researcher?", p: "\"Please don't present that slide until Thursday.\"" },
    { s: "What's an invention disclosure meeting?", p: "Where engineers say 'it's nothing special' about the thing most worth patenting." },
    { s: "What's the most dangerous phrase in a specification?", p: "\"The present invention is...\" Everything after it becomes a limitation." },
    { s: "Why did the drafter write 'in some embodiments' 412 times?", p: "Because 'always' is a claim construction trap." },
    { s: "What's a patent drafter's favorite punctuation mark?", p: "The semicolon. Every limitation deserves a little pause;" },
    { s: "What's the longest sentence ever written by a lawyer?", p: "Claim 1. A preamble, eight elements, one period and no regrets." },
    { s: "What did the transitional phrase say to the preamble?", p: "\"You set the stage. I decide who gets in.\"" },
    { s: "Why did the Markman hearing feel like a vocabulary test?", p: "Three days arguing about whether 'coupled' means touching." },
    { s: "What does 'coupled' mean?", p: "Depends on which side of the 'v.' you're standing on." },
    { s: "Why did the expert witness bring a dictionary to the deposition?", p: "Plain and ordinary meaning had never seemed so extraordinary." },
    { s: "What's a reasonable royalty?", p: "The number both sides agree is unreasonable." },
    { s: "What's the entire market value rule?", p: "Asking whether your patented button really sells the whole car." },
    { s: "Why did the patentee start marking products?", p: "No marking, no notice, no pre-suit damages. Stickers are cheap." },
    { s: "What does 'patent pending' actually protect?", p: "Morale, mostly. Enforceable rights wait for the grant." },
    { s: "What's a declaratory judgment action?", p: "Answering a demand letter by suing first." },
    { s: "What's the all-elements rule?", p: "Missing one ingredient means it isn't your recipe." },
    { s: "Why did the accused infringer feel safe?", p: "Prosecution history estoppel. The patentee gave that range away years ago." },
    { s: "What's prosecution history estoppel?", p: "Anything you say to the examiner can and will be used against you." },
    { s: "Why did the prosecution attorney get a call from the litigation team?", p: "Someone found the phrase 'the invention requires' in an old response." },
    { s: "What did the patent owner's response say about the petition?", p: "\"Petitioner has combined two references that clearly can't stand each other.\"" },
    { s: "What's the most anticipated document in an IPR?", p: "The institution decision. Months of waiting for one word." },
    { s: "What's a motion to amend at the PTAB?", p: "Trying to remodel the house while it's being inspected." },
    { s: "Why did the patent survive the IPR?", p: "The petitioner's best reference turned out not to be a printed publication." },
    { s: "What's de novo review?", p: "The appeals court saying, 'Let's look at this with fresh eyes. Ours.'" },
    { s: "Why did the examiner count to 20?", p: "Excess claim fees start at 21." },
    { s: "Why did the client want 60 claims?", p: "Then they saw the excess claims fee and found the 20 they loved most." },
    { s: "What's a multiple dependent claim?", p: "A claim that depends on several others, and is priced accordingly." },
    { s: "Why did the inventor call the attorney at midnight?", p: "They'd just remembered a second embodiment. And a third." },
    { s: "Why can't the claim just say 'everything'?", p: "Because 'everything' is anticipated by everything." },
    { s: "What did the inventor say after reading the claims?", p: "\"That's not my invention.\" The attorney: \"Exactly. It's broader.\"" },
    { s: "Why do inventors find claims so unromantic?", p: "They wrote a symphony; the claims call it 'a plurality of sounds.'" },
    { s: "What does 'a plurality' mean?", p: "Two or more. Lawyers just needed it to sound more expensive." },
    { s: "Why did the patent get a sequel?", p: "The continuation had all the same characters and a narrower plot." },
    { s: "Why did the competitor monitor the pending continuation?", p: "Its claims kept quietly drifting toward their product." },
    { s: "What's a patent portfolio review?", p: "Sorting the crown jewels from the costume jewelry." },
    { s: "Why did the portfolio manager let 40 patents lapse?", p: "The maintenance fees were more than the products earned." },
    { s: "Why did the office action response take three months?", p: "Two months of thinking, one week of drafting and three weeks of client approval." },
    { s: "Why do extension-of-time fees exist?", p: "So procrastination has a price list." },
    { s: "What's a docketing error's natural predator?", p: "A second reviewer." },
    { s: "What's the grace period outside the US?", p: "Mostly a rumor." },
    { s: "Why did the inventor regret the social media post?", p: "Absolute-novelty countries read it too." },
    { s: "What's an inventorship dispute?", p: "Everyone in the meeting remembers having the idea first." },
    { s: "Why wasn't the project manager named as an inventor?", p: "Saying 'make it faster' isn't conception." },
    { s: "Why did the employment agreement matter so much?", p: "It said 'hereby assigns,' not 'agrees to assign.' Two words, entire lawsuits." },
    { s: "Why did due diligence find a gap in the chain of title?", p: "One assignment was signed, scanned and never recorded." },
    { s: "What's a field-of-use restriction?", p: "You can use it in toasters. Not in spaceships." },
    { s: "Why did the licensor audit the royalty reports?", p: "The 'net sales' definition ran three pages." },
    { s: "What did the function-way-result test ask?", p: "Does it walk like the claim, swim like the claim and quack like the claim?" },
    { s: "Why did the algorithm hire a lawyer?", p: "It wanted to be patentable, but everyone kept calling it math." },
    { s: "What did the law of nature say to the inventor?", p: "\"You found me. That doesn't mean you made me.\"" },
    { s: "Why can't you patent a mineral found in the ground?", p: "Discovering it isn't inventing it. Nature had priority." },
    { s: "Why does a design patent have only one claim?", p: "Because a picture is worth a thousand limitations." },
    { s: "What's a design patent's favorite line?", p: "The broken line. It means 'not claimed, but nice to look at.'" },
    { s: "What's a plant patent?", p: "The only patent you have to water." },
    { s: "What's an examiner's favorite puzzle?", p: "Assembling your invention from four references without the picture on the box." },
    { s: "What's 'motivation to combine'?", p: "A reason to mix two references, other than the examiner really wanting to." },
    { s: "Why did the attorney argue non-analogous art?", p: "A fishing-lure patent isn't the first place you look to fix a semiconductor." },
    { s: "What did the examiner say about the unexpected results?", p: "\"I'd be surprised, if you'd shown me any data.\"" },
    { s: "What's official notice?", p: "The examiner saying 'everyone knows this.' Your move: 'Please prove it.'" },
    { s: "Why did the examiner and attorney finally agree?", p: "During the interview, they realized they'd been reading different versions of claim 1." },
    { s: "What's the patent bar exam?", p: "Six hours of open-book questions that make you wish the book were shorter." },
    { s: "Why did the new associate carry the MPEP everywhere?", p: "Arm day, every day." },
    { s: "What's a patent attorney's version of a vacation?", p: "Responding to office actions from a different time zone." },
    { s: "Why did the patent need a certificate of correction?", p: "Claim 1 depended on claim 1." },
    { s: "What's a sequence listing?", p: "A biotech application's soundtrack, in XML." },
    { s: "Why do chemists love Markush groups?", p: "One claim, ten million compounds and zero nights of sleep for the examiner." },
    { s: "What's a genus claim?", p: "Claiming the whole forest after planting three trees." },
    { s: "Why did the patent attorney name the dog 'Embodiment'?", p: "So there would always be at least one example." },
    { s: "Why did the cat sit on the draft application?", p: "To establish prior possession." },
    { s: "Why did the patent attorney order a sandwich 'consisting of' bread and cheese?", p: "No surprise ingredients. Closed claim, closed sandwich." },
    { s: "Why did the patent attorney's coffee order take ten minutes?", p: "They asked for it 'substantially hot,' and the barista wanted a numerical range." },
    { s: "How do patent attorneys introduce themselves?", p: "\"Hi, I'm, in some embodiments, a patent attorney.\"" },
    { s: "How does a patent attorney sign a birthday card?", p: "\"Best wishes, without limitation.\"" },
    { s: "What did the patent attorney name the sailboat?", p: "The Whereby." },
    { s: "What's IP due diligence?", p: "Finding out what the company owns, versus what it thinks it owns." },
    { s: "Why was the open-source license such a surprise?", p: "The 'free' library came with obligations nobody read." },
    { s: "What's fair use?", p: "The four-factor test everyone is certain they pass." },
    { s: "What did the examiner say when told the reference was too old?", p: "\"Prior art doesn't expire. It just gets dustier.\"" },
    { s: "Why did a patent from the 1890s get cited last week?", p: "Turns out the steam-powered version anticipated the app." },
    { s: "What's the oldest trick in the examiner's book?", p: "Inherency: 'It must have done that, even if nobody noticed.'" },
    { s: "Why does the patent team read the Official Gazette every Tuesday?", p: "Patents issue on Tuesdays. So does the anxiety." },
    { s: "What did the response say to the office action?", p: "\"Respectfully, the Office has misunderstood. Respectfully.\"" },
    { s: "Why do responses begin with 'Applicant thanks the Examiner'?", p: "Tradition. And because the next 30 pages are disagreement." },
    { s: "Why did the attorney elect with traverse?", p: "Pick one, protest anyway. It's tradition." },
    { s: "What's an Ex parte Quayle action?", p: "Allowed, except for the paperwork. So close you can taste it." },
    { s: "Why did the application wait 22 months for a first action?", p: "It was in a very popular art unit." },
    { s: "What's a defensive patent?", p: "One you file hoping you never need it." },
    { s: "Why did the patent sit on the shelf for 15 years?", p: "It was waiting for the market to catch up to claim 3." },
    { s: "What's a patent attorney's favorite magic words?", p: "\"Configured to.\" They turn any box into structure." },
    { s: "What's the difference between 'configured to' and 'capable of'?", p: "About two years of litigation." },
    { s: "Why did the drafter include a 40-page glossary?", p: "They had been to a Markman hearing before." },
    { s: "What's a picture claim?", p: "A claim so detailed it describes exactly one product. Yours." },
    { s: "What's a claim tree?", p: "The only tree in a law firm that grows from the top down." },
    { s: "What did the independent claim tell its dependents?", p: "\"If I'm infringed, you might be too. If I fall, you might still stand.\"" },
    { s: "Why do IP lawyers appreciate origami?", p: "Folding one idea into twelve embodiments is basically the job." },
    { s: "What did the examiner say about the perpetual motion machine?", p: "\"Rejected for lack of utility. Also, physics.\"" },
    { s: "Why was the time machine application rejected?", p: "The examiner found prior art filed next Thursday." },
    { s: "Why was the invisibility cloak application rejected?", p: "The drawings didn't show anything." },
    { s: "Why did the teleporter application get a restriction requirement?", p: "Departure and arrival were found to be two distinct inventions." },
    { s: "Why was the mind-reading device rejected?", p: "The examiner already knew what it claimed." },
    { s: "What did the inventor of the better mousetrap learn?", p: "The world beat a path to the door, and so did three prior-art references." },
    { s: "What did the patent say to the trade secret?", p: "\"I disclosed everything for 20 years. You disclosed nothing, forever. Who's winning?\"" },
    { s: "Why did the 'comprising' claim welcome the extra feature?", p: "Adding a cupholder doesn't avoid infringement." },
    { s: "What's a royalty stack?", p: "When every patent wants a slice, and the pie is a $3 component." },
    { s: "What do patent attorneys and archaeologists have in common?", p: "Both get excited about old documents proving someone was there first." },
    { s: "What do patent drafters and poets have in common?", p: "Every word counts. Only one of them gets cross-examined on it." },
    { s: "Why was the IP department's holiday party so quiet?", p: "Nobody wanted to publicly disclose anything." },
    { s: "Why couldn't the inventor bring a guest to the prototype demo?", p: "Without an NDA, a guest is an audience." },
    { s: "What's the e-filing system's busiest minute?", p: "11:59 p.m. Eastern. Everyone visits." },
    { s: "Why did the filing upload fail at 11:58 p.m.?", p: "The PDF had a font that wasn't embedded." },
    { s: "What did the paralegal say at 11:57 p.m.?", p: "\"The filing receipt just came through. Everyone can breathe.\"" },
    { s: "Why does 'for at least the reasons above' appear so often?", p: "It turns one argument into twenty dependent-claim arguments." },
    { s: "What does 'Applicant reserves the right' really mean?", p: "\"We're not arguing that now, but don't get comfortable.\"" },
    { s: "What happens to canceled claims?", p: "They wait patiently in a continuation, plotting a comeback." },
    { s: "What did the prosecutor tell the litigator?", p: "\"I wrote it broad.\" The litigator replied: \"I'll defend it narrow.\"" },
    { s: "Why did the verdict form run 12 pages?", p: "Every claim, every patent, every defense and one small box for damages." },
    { s: "Why did the startup frame its first patent?", p: "Investors love patents. A frame makes them look even more expensive." },
    { s: "Why did the claim chart need a sixth column?", p: "The accused product had five elements. The claim had six." },
  ];

  // ── Content: Patent of the Day — real patents, famous and absurd ──
  var PATENTS = [
    { no: "US 6,469", y: 1849, t: "Buoying Vessels Over Shoals", s: "Abraham Lincoln's device for lifting riverboats off sandbars — making him the only US president with a patent. It was never manufactured, but the model he whittled is in the Smithsonian." },
    { no: "US 174,465", y: 1876, t: "Improvement in Telegraphy", s: "Alexander Graham Bell's telephone patent — often called the most valuable patent ever issued. Bell's filing famously beat Elisha Gray's caveat for a similar invention at the Patent Office the very same day." },
    { no: "US 223,898", y: 1880, t: "Electric Lamp", s: "Edison's incandescent bulb with a carbonized filament. The patent survived years of litigation that established Edison didn't invent the lightbulb so much as the first one that actually worked — and the system to power it." },
    { no: "US 2,717,437", y: 1955, t: "Velvet Type Fabric (Velcro)", s: "George de Mestral examined the burrs stuck to his dog after a walk under a microscope, saw thousands of tiny hooks, and spent a decade making 'hook and loop' fasteners practical. The patent expired in 1978 — which is why everyone makes it now." },
    { no: "US 2,415,012", y: 1947, t: "Toy and Process of Use (Slinky)", s: "Naval engineer Richard James watched a torsion spring walk off a shelf and patented a 'helical spring toy.' Three hundred million Slinkys later, it's a lesson in claiming the use, not just the thing." },
    { no: "US 3,691,140", y: 1972, t: "Acrylate Copolymer Microspheres (Post-it)", s: "Spencer Silver patented a 'low-tack' adhesive nobody at 3M could find a use for — until Art Fry needed a bookmark that wouldn't fall out of his hymnal. The product launched twelve years after the patent." },
    { no: "US 3,351,836", y: 1967, t: "Intermittent Windshield Wiper", s: "Robert Kearns pitched his wiper to Ford, was turned away, then watched it appear on their cars. His decades-long infringement crusade won ~$30M and became the film 'Flash of Genius' — the patent system's great cautionary epic." },
    { no: "US 5,960,411", y: 1999, t: "1-Click Ordering", s: "Amazon's patent on buying with a single click — the poster child of the business-method patent era. Apple licensed it for the iTunes store rather than fight. It expired in 2017, and 'Buy Now' buttons bloomed everywhere overnight." },
    { no: "US 6,004,596", y: 1999, t: "Sealed Crustless Sandwich", s: "Smucker's patent behind Uncrustables. When they sued a small Michigan grocer over crimped PB&J, the resulting reexamination shredded the claims — prior art included, roughly, every grandmother." },
    { no: "AU 2001100012", y: 2001, t: "Circular Transportation Facilitation Device", s: "Australian patent attorney John Keogh patented the wheel to prove the new unexamined 'innovation patent' system would grant anything. IP Australia was not amused; the Ig Nobel committee was." },
    { no: "US 221,855", y: 1879, t: "Improvement in Fire-Escapes", s: "A parachute worn as a hat, paired with padded overshoes for landing. Benjamin Oppenheimer's drawing of a serene gentleman plummeting past a burning building remains one of the Patent Office's great artworks." },
    { no: "US 5,443,036", y: 1995, t: "Method of Exercising a Cat", s: "A granted patent on pointing a laser at the floor so a cat chases the dot. Exhibit A in every conversation about examination quality — and prior art known to every cat owner since the invention of the flashlight." },
    { no: "US 4,022,227", y: 1977, t: "Method of Concealing Partial Baldness", s: "A father-and-son team patented the comb-over: divide the remaining hair into three sections, sweep strategically. It won an Ig Nobel and immortality in §101 lectures." },
    { no: "US 6,360,693", y: 2002, t: "Animal Toy", s: "An 'apparatus for animal amusement' that is, on any fair reading of the claims, a stick. The USPTO later ordered reexamination. Dogs declined to comment on the validity of the prior art." },
    { no: "US 6,637,447", y: 2003, t: "Beerbrella", s: "A miniature umbrella that clips to a beer bottle to shade your beverage. Claim 1 is fully enabled, entirely novel at filing, and a standing reminder that patentable and profitable are different questions." },
    { no: "US 748,626", y: 1904, t: "The Landlord's Game", s: "Lizzie Magie patented the board game that became Monopoly — designed, ironically, to teach the evils of monopolies. Parker Brothers later bought her patent for about $500 and credited Charles Darrow instead." },
    { no: "US 72,X", y: 1794, t: "Cotton Gin", s: "Eli Whitney's patent for separating cotton fiber from seed reshaped the American South's economy — and was infringed so widely that Whitney barely made a dollar from it. The lesson every startup relearns: a patent you can't enforce is just paper." },
    { no: "US 3,633", y: 1844, t: "The Vulcanization of India-Rubber", s: "Charles Goodyear stumbled onto sulfur-and-heat vulcanization in a stove accident. He spent his life in and out of debtor's prison enforcing the patent, dying $200,000 in debt. The tire company that bore his name was founded decades after his death." },
    { no: "US 8,294", y: 1851, t: "Improvement in Fastening for Suspender-Ends", s: "Elias Howe's separate patent covering a slide-fastener concept anticipated the zipper by decades. Gideon Sundback perfected the modern zipper in 1913 — a classic case of patented, unmarketed, and eclipsed." },
    { no: "US 6,281", y: 1849, t: "Improvement in Safety Elevators", s: "Elisha Otis's safety brake — a spring-loaded ratchet that clamped the guide rails if the cable snapped — didn't sell until he cut the rope himself at the 1854 World's Fair. Skyscrapers followed." },
    { no: "US 78,317", y: 1868, t: "Type-Writing Machine", s: "Christopher Sholes's QWERTY layout was designed to separate common letter pairs so mechanical typebars wouldn't jam. The keyboard survived the jam problem by a century — and every device since." },
    { no: "US 78,015", y: 1868, t: "Wire-Fence", s: "Joseph Glidden's barbed wire ended the open-range West more effectively than any legislation. His victory over rival Isaac Ellwood cemented one of the most-litigated patents in US history." },
    { no: "US 174,465", y: 1876, t: "Improvement in Telegraphy — Bell's Second Patent", s: "The 1876 filing wasn't Bell's only telephone patent; US 186,787 (1877) covered the receiver. Both survived hundreds of infringement suits — the most successful patent enforcement campaign of the 19th century." },
    { no: "US 200,521", y: 1878, t: "Phonograph or Speaking-Machine", s: "Edison's foil-cylinder recorder played back the human voice — the first machine that could. He listed telephone-message dictation and 'the record of dying utterances of family members' among its uses." },
    { no: "US 227,229", y: 1880, t: "Method of Preparing Photographic Emulsion", s: "George Eastman's dry-plate gelatin process replaced messy wet-collodion photography. He built the Kodak empire around a slogan he trademarked before the camera even shipped: 'You press the button, we do the rest.'" },
    { no: "US 335,159", y: 1886, t: "System of Electrical Distribution", s: "Nikola Tesla's polyphase AC system won the War of the Currents. Westinghouse bought the patents; Tesla later tore up his royalty contract to save the company from bankruptcy — walking away from what would have been billions." },
    { no: "US 388,170", y: 1888, t: "Alternating-Current Motor", s: "Tesla's induction motor patent — the invention that made AC power distribution practical. Every appliance with an AC motor still traces to this filing." },
    { no: "US 549,160", y: 1895, t: "Gas Engine", s: "The Selden patent claimed to cover any gasoline-powered automobile. Ford spent eight years and hundreds of thousands fighting it — and won on appeal in 1911, freeing the American auto industry from a nonpracticing entity a century before the term existed." },
    { no: "US 586,193", y: 1897, t: "Transmission of Signals by Wireless", s: "Marconi's radiotelegraphy patent. The Supreme Court revoked its US validity in 1943 (Marconi Wireless v. US), holding Tesla, Lodge, and Stone had prior art — one of the great posthumous vindications in patent history." },
    { no: "US 613,809", y: 1898, t: "Method of and Apparatus for Controlling Mechanism of Moving Vessels", s: "Tesla's demonstration of a radio-controlled boat at Madison Square Garden. He proposed it as a torpedo. Nobody funded it. The technology waited half a century for the drone age." },
    { no: "US 686,046", y: 1901, t: "Automobile with Front-Wheel Drive", s: "Walter Christie's front-wheel-drive car patent influenced automotive engineering for decades. Christie's later T3 tank suspension design fed directly into the Soviet T-34 — the paper trail runs through Detroit to Stalingrad." },
    { no: "US 745,157", y: 1903, t: "Ferric-Aluminum Alloy — Radar Foundations", s: "Christian Hülsmeyer's Telemobiloscope was the first radio-echo obstacle detector — the direct ancestor of radar. His demonstrations impressed no one. Radar was reinvented, urgently, in the late 1930s." },
    { no: "US 821,393", y: 1906, t: "Flying Machine", s: "The Wright brothers' patent claimed 'wing-warping' for lateral control. Curtiss's ailerons became the workaround — and the case dragged on until WWI forced a government-mandated patent pool in 1917 to make American planes possible." },
    { no: "US 942,809", y: 1909, t: "Manufacture of Radiators", s: "Willis Carrier's air-conditioning patents (this one and US 1,172,784) — the invention that made summer productivity possible, movie theaters profitable, and the American South economically viable at scale." },
    { no: "US 1,242,872", y: 1917, t: "Fastener", s: "Gideon Sundback's 'Separable Fastener' — the modern zipper. It took another decade before B.F. Goodrich named it 'zipper' after the sound their overshoes made. Language followed function." },
    { no: "US 1,203,190", y: 1916, t: "Sanitary Method for Preserving Milk", s: "Clarence Birdseye's flash-freezing patents transformed food preservation. He sold the technology to General Foods for $22 million in 1929 — a fortune that Depression-era investors nearly forced him to accept in stock." },
    { no: "US 1,647,209", y: 1927, t: "Airplane", s: "Charles Lindbergh's Spirit of St. Louis wasn't patented, but Ryan Airlines patented refinements. The plane's fame outstripped every intellectual-property document ever filed on it." },
    { no: "US 1,773,980", y: 1930, t: "Television System", s: "Philo Farnsworth's electronic television — the man RCA fought for a decade rather than pay. He finally collected a million-dollar royalty in 1939, after which David Sarnoff's marketing machine buried his name in favor of Zworykin's." },
    { no: "US 1,745,175", y: 1930, t: "Method of Manufacturing Electric Capacitors", s: "Julius Lilienfeld's transistor concept — 17 years before Bell Labs. Lilienfeld's patent used solid-state control of current, but no working device was built. Bell Labs' 1947 breakthrough had to design around it." },
    { no: "US 2,140,132", y: 1938, t: "Fluorescent Lamp", s: "Edmund Germer's high-pressure fluorescent tube — GE bought his patent and made the fluorescent lamp a global standard. Every office in the 20th century paid Germer, indirectly." },
    { no: "US 2,168,703", y: 1939, t: "Fabric-Like Material — Nylon", s: "Wallace Carothers's polyamide fiber patent (with DuPont) launched synthetic fabrics. Carothers died before nylon stockings sold their first pair; his suicide two years before the product launch is one of the tragic footnotes of chemistry." },
    { no: "US 2,158,564", y: 1939, t: "Chair", s: "Charles Eames's molded plywood chair — patented before he became a design icon. Herman Miller has been paying variations of this royalty for 80 years." },
    { no: "US 2,292,387", y: 1942, t: "Secret Communication System", s: "Hedy Lamarr and George Antheil's frequency-hopping patent for radio-controlled torpedoes. The Navy shelved it as 'too complicated.' Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, and CDMA rediscovered it half a century later — none of which paid the estate a dime." },
    { no: "US 2,297,691", y: 1942, t: "Electrophotography", s: "Chester Carlson's xerography patent. Twenty companies rejected it. Haloid bought it in 1947, renamed itself Xerox in 1961, and became one of the most valuable patent bets in industrial history." },
    { no: "US 2,435,486", y: 1948, t: "Microwave Cooking Apparatus", s: "Percy Spencer, a Raytheon engineer, noticed a chocolate bar melting in his pocket near a magnetron. The first commercial Radarange stood six feet tall and cost the equivalent of $50,000. Countertop models took another 20 years." },
    { no: "US 2,524,035", y: 1950, t: "Three-Electrode Circuit Element — Transistor", s: "William Shockley's junction transistor patent — the device that displaced the vacuum tube and made every subsequent semiconductor. Bardeen and Brattain got the point-contact patent (US 2,524,033); the trio shared the 1956 Nobel Prize." },
    { no: "US 2,569,347", y: 1951, t: "Photography by Reflection", s: "Edwin Land's instant photography — the Polaroid patent. The company built an empire on cameras that developed prints in sixty seconds. When Kodak launched a competing instant camera in 1976, Land sued and won $909 million — the largest patent verdict of its era." },
    { no: "US 2,714,890", y: 1955, t: "Zipper-Type Package Closure", s: "Rip 'n' Zip: the resealable plastic bag closure. Robbins and Fischer's design won the war of the zip-top bag, and eventually became the Ziploc that made Dow Chemical billions." },
    { no: "US 2,752,624", y: 1956, t: "Bar Code Reader", s: "Joseph Woodland and Bernard Silver's original bar-code patent (bull's-eye version). Retail waited two decades for lasers and computer processing cheap enough to make it work. Woodland got his Medal of Technology in 1992 — Silver had died in 1963." },
    { no: "US 3,073,516", y: 1963, t: "Miniaturized Electronic Circuit", s: "Jack Kilby's integrated circuit patent for Texas Instruments. Robert Noyce's competing IC patent (US 2,981,877) went to Fairchild Semiconductor. The two shared credit — and Kilby got the 2000 Nobel Prize after Noyce's death made him ineligible." },
    { no: "US 3,138,743", y: 1964, t: "Miniaturized Electronic Circuits — Noyce", s: "Robert Noyce's monolithic integrated circuit — the planar process that made Silicon Valley possible. Intel, Fairchild, and every semiconductor company since owe him." },
    { no: "US 3,150,232", y: 1964, t: "Portable Radio Transceiver", s: "Motorola's handheld radio patent, the seed of the mobile phone. Martin Cooper's 1973 demo call from a Manhattan sidewalk to Bell Labs was made on Motorola's DynaTAC prototype — a two-pound brick that became a category." },
    { no: "US 3,449,750", y: 1969, t: "Duplex Digital Data Communication System", s: "The modem patent by John van Geen and John Rife. Every dial-up connection paid tribute to this filing, all the way to the fax machine's final day." },
    { no: "US 3,541,541", y: 1970, t: "X-Y Position Indicator for a Display System", s: "Doug Engelbart's computer mouse patent — filed in 1967, granted in 1970. Stanford Research Institute licensed it to Apple for $40,000. The mouse became universal; SRI made no fortune." },
    { no: "US 3,713,148", y: 1973, t: "Transponder Apparatus and System — RFID Origins", s: "Mario Cardullo's active RFID transponder patent — the ancestor of every keyless entry, contactless card, and inventory tag on Earth." },
    { no: "US 3,748,632", y: 1973, t: "Compressor Apparatus — Rotary Compressor", s: "The scroll compressor that changed HVAC. Every efficient air conditioner since owes performance to this invention — one of those quiet patents that reshaped an industry without headlines." },
    { no: "US 3,825,995", y: 1974, t: "Method for Cooking with Fluidic Beam", s: "Amana's microwave oven patents made countertop microwaves affordable. By 1986, more American kitchens had microwaves than dishwashers." },
    { no: "US 4,022,879", y: 1977, t: "Method for Solving Rubik's Cube", s: "Ernő Rubik's original Hungarian patent predated foreign filings; Ideal Toy Corp. bought the US rights and moved 200 million units by 1983. The math community couldn't stop playing." },
    { no: "US 4,063,220", y: 1977, t: "Multipoint Data Communication System", s: "Robert Metcalfe's Ethernet patent for Xerox PARC. The 3Com company he founded around it turned a Xerox side project into a networking standard. Ethernet still ships every day." },
    { no: "US 4,146,882", y: 1979, t: "Digital Notepad", s: "The stylus-and-tablet interface that anticipated the iPad by three decades. Multiple attempts to commercialize it flopped. Apple's Newton (1993) got closer. The iPhone finally made touch mainstream." },
    { no: "US 4,237,224", y: 1980, t: "Process for Producing Biologically Functional Molecular Chimeras", s: "Cohen and Boyer's recombinant DNA patent. Stanford licensed it non-exclusively; over 400 companies signed up. It made $255 million and helped birth the biotech industry." },
    { no: "US 4,259,444", y: 1981, t: "Microorganism for Digesting Oil", s: "Ananda Chakrabarty's genetically-engineered oil-eating bacterium — the invention behind Diamond v. Chakrabarty. Life-forms, patentable if human-made. Modern biotech began the day this reissued." },
    { no: "US 4,405,829", y: 1983, t: "Cryptographic Communications System", s: "The RSA public-key encryption patent (Rivest, Shamir, Adleman). It expired in September 2000; the algorithm was released for free two weeks early. Modern e-commerce runs on the mathematics of this filing." },
    { no: "US 4,458,066", y: 1984, t: "Method for Producing Polynucleotides — PCR", s: "Kary Mullis's polymerase chain reaction — the invention that made molecular biology industrial. Cetus sold the rights to Roche for $300 million in 1991. Mullis got the 1993 Nobel Prize." },
    { no: "US 4,528,643", y: 1985, t: "System for Reproducing Information in Material Objects at a Point of Sale Location", s: "Rick Freeman's kiosk-based digital retail patent — the seed of every download-in-store service, later Cliffs Notes, then iTunes-in-a-box concepts. Ahead of its time by two decades." },
    { no: "US 4,608,455", y: 1986, t: "Bilateral Filament — Kevlar", s: "Stephanie Kwolek's DuPont patent for para-aramid fiber. Bulletproof vests, tires, sailcloth, spacecraft — all Kevlar. Kwolek discovered it looking for a lightweight tire fiber during the 1965 oil crisis." },
    { no: "US 4,742,559", y: 1988, t: "Cellular Radio Telephone System", s: "AT&T's cellular telephone system patent. Every carrier on Earth built variations of this architecture. It expired without fanfare; the industry it enabled had moved on to 4G." },
    { no: "US 4,910,732", y: 1990, t: "Onset of GPS Civilian Use", s: "Charles Trimble's GPS receiver patent — the invention that took a Cold War satellite constellation and turned it into car navigation. Trimble Navigation still exists on the strength of it." },
    { no: "US 5,051,745", y: 1991, t: "Optical Recording Medium", s: "The DVD's underlying optical-storage patents. Toshiba, Panasonic, Philips, and Sony pooled dozens of these — the DVD Consortium became a case study in coordinated cross-licensing." },
    { no: "US 5,132,992", y: 1992, t: "Anti-Collision System for Vehicles", s: "The radar-based automotive collision-avoidance patent that Mercedes eventually shipped in the S-Class. The idea outran the sensor cost by 20 years." },
    { no: "US 5,175,571", y: 1992, t: "Glueless Adhesion for Books", s: "Peter Duke's perfect-binding patent — the reason every mass-market paperback holds together. Book manufacturing shifted permanently the year this deployed." },
    { no: "US 5,278,860", y: 1994, t: "Modulation for Transmitting Digital Signals", s: "The QAM modulation scheme that powered cable modems, DSL, and every subsequent broadband technology. A quiet mathematics-heavy filing that shaped the internet." },
    { no: "US 5,341,293", y: 1994, t: "User Interface for a Mobile Device", s: "The original PalmPilot handwriting-recognition (Graffiti) patent. Xerox eventually sued and won a partial victory. Palm settled by moving to Graffiti 2 — which nobody liked." },
    { no: "US 5,404,505", y: 1995, t: "Hyperlink Method", s: "One of many hyperlink-related patents that emerged as the Web exploded. Most enforcement attempts failed under prior art; Berners-Lee's philosophy of open standards was the era's dominant defense." },
    { no: "US 5,432,867", y: 1995, t: "Method for Cellular Digital Camera", s: "The first cellular-camera integration patent — years before flip phones with cameras hit consumers. The patent aged into gold as camera phones became universal." },
